00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:13.360 align:start position:0%
Resident Evil seven has been heralded as  a return to form a classic  uncompromising fright fest and indeed it  is in place of pandering blockbuster  action the game delivers intimate horror  with a highly focused scope and all the  trappings you'd expect from a survival  game no superfluous skill trees or  meaningless fetch quests just the  stomach churning tension of guiding a  relatively helpless character out of a  waking nightmare every time you start to  feel powerful enough to kick some ass  the game finds a way to pull the rug out  from under and while the setting and  characters have no obvious connections  to any previous Resident Evil games the  series DNA can be felt in everything  from the puzzles and keys to the subtle  psychological tricks used to cultivate  dread you're about to see some more but  rv7 clearly takes cues from modern  horror games as well most notably by  opting for a first-person perspective  outlast fans may recognize a lot of  structural similarities in large  portions of the campaign which outside  of some optional backtracking to collect  items follows a linear and highly  scripted series of events annoyingly  that sometimes means wasting ammo on  enemies who are not supposed to die  until later fights but at least those  moments reinforce the tense oppressive  atmosphere that persists from start to  finish the experience does start to drag  a bit by the end though as you end up  performing similar tasks throughout the  game but make no mistake there are  memorable moments and when they hit they  hit hard  despite its clarity of vision including  restraint in its pursuit of that vision  re7 still takes a few bold chances to  refresh the campaign and renew the  horror as you progress  where do you think you're going some of  these chances stem from its story which  follows average guy ethan winters into a  dilapidated manor deep in the Louisiana  bayou though ethan searching for his  missing wife the narrative never  devolves into trite damsel in distress  cliches over all the narrative delivers  the twists are generally predictable but  it's exciting to see them unfold and the  game never lingers on a reveal for too  long the setting helps as well every  mouldy floorboard every flickering light  every losing end trail feels unbearably  the textures details and sounds are  without exception utterly gross in the  best way and impressively re7 relies far  more on its atmosphere than cheap jump  scares  [Applause]  re7 also smartly uses each family member  to different effect the father Jack  provides in-your-face terror the mother  Marguerite demands a stealthier approach  and the son Lucas sets elaborate  saw-like traps that keep you off balance  and make you wary of things you once  took for granted these different  approaches add welcome variety in a way  that feels completely natural in the  context of the world enhancing your  immersion while mixing up the gameplay  outside of the family  collectible VHS tapes add even more  variety each one contains a playable  flashback from a different characters  perspective which works beautifully as  both a narrative device and a way to  break up Ethan's exhausting mission  you'll also encounter puzzles though  unlike early Resident Evil games these  play only a minor role overall and most  are simple and easy to solve there are  of course a few things that go bump in  the night as well and for those  occasions Ethan has access to a small  but diverse collection of weapons the  game contains more guns than I expected  but I never felt super powered not even  close even the final chapter mainly  relies on horror and tension by and  large re7 keeps it simple  prioritizing tone over action and in  true survival horror fashion everything  is in short supply you'll definitely  need to manage your limited inventory  and carefully scour areas for essential  items unfortunately I found most generic  enemies to be a little less capable than  I would have liked if they catch you off  guard or confront you in a bottleneck  they can be tough to put down but I  could frequently see them coming and  they're neither fast nor smart enough to  catch you when you simply run the other  direction knowing when to run away is  arguably part of any horror game but I  still would have preferred something  more threatening especially since I was  playing on the highest difficulty  available out of the box boss fights  were also a bit of a mix one thrilling  battle recaptures the classic resident  evil boss designed by throwing you in a  creepy intricate arena but another more  action-oriented fight forced me to win  in a specific way that wasn't  immediately  clearly thankfully the games generally  pretty generous when you die responding  you nearby with all your ammo and  healing items intact also if you own a  PS VR system this game does everything  it can to deliver a top tier experience  including a robust set of options  designed to minimize discomfort while I  can't imagine playing the entire 12 hour  campaign with a headset on re7 is  undoubtedly an amazing option for VR  fans by the end of the campaign I was  ready for the game to be over but that's  okay re7 ends just as it starts the odd  States welcome and after the fact I felt  like I'd survived a truly harrowing  journey the boss fights maybe slightly  inconsistent and certain sections might  drag after a while but re7  executes its overall vision with  impressive patience and precision by  returning to horror Resident Evil has  once again become something special  [Music]
 

